{
  "pages": [
    {
      "page_idx": 5,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "bbox": [
            0.119,
            0.072,
            0.159,
            0.087
          ],
          "content": "128"
        },
        {
          "type": "header",
          "bbox": [
            0.187,
            0.071,
            0.494,
            0.089
          ],
          "content": "Ganita Manjari | Grade 9 | Part II"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.115,
            0.11,
            0.883,
            0.168
          ],
          "content": "When doctors take an MRI scan of some part of your body, it is like taking a cross-section because it allows them to look at the inside of your body in slices."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.152,
            0.168,
            0.719,
            0.187
          ],
          "content": "What is the area of the curved surface of a cylinder?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.115,
            0.187,
            0.882,
            0.245
          ],
          "content": "Let the radius of the circular base of the cylinder be $r ;$ let its height be h. Suppose the cylinder is cut as shown in the figure below. We get a rectangle."
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.189,
            0.256,
            0.316,
            0.379
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_5_image_5.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.378,
            0.256,
            0.537,
            0.382
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [
            {
              "bbox": [
                0.448,
                0.397,
                0.549,
                0.417
              ],
              "content": "Fig. 14.5A"
            }
          ],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_5_image_6.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.597,
            0.271,
            0.813,
            0.379
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_5_image_8.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.116,
            0.426,
            0.881,
            0.466
          ],
          "content": "Therefore, the curved surface area of the cylinder is the area of the rectangle."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.153,
            0.471,
            0.703,
            0.49
          ],
          "content": "• The curved surface area (CSA) of the cylinder is"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.298,
            0.495,
            0.698,
            0.515
          ],
          "content": "\\mathrm{CSA}=\\mathrm{circun~free~nc~} \\times \\mathrm{height}=2 \\pi r h.",
          "image_source": "images/page_5_equation_11.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.116,
            0.521,
            0.688,
            0.598
          ],
          "content": "If the cylinder is closed at both ends (top and bottom) like a biscuit tin (see Fig. 14.5B), then the flat circular surfaces at the ends also contribute to the area. Since there are two ends,"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.199,
            0.602,
            0.601,
            0.641
          ],
          "content": "total surface area $(TSA) = 2\\pi r h + 2\\pi r^{2} = 2\\pi r(h + r).$"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.708,
            0.522,
            0.895,
            0.642
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_5_image_14.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.153,
            0.652,
            0.685,
            0.691
          ],
          "content": "If the cylinder is closed at one end and open at the other end, like a tumbler, then"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.753,
            0.658,
            0.851,
            0.677
          ],
          "content": "Fig. 14.5B"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.298,
            0.696,
            0.698,
            0.715
          ],
          "content": "{ \\mathrm { s u r f a } } { \\overline { { \\mathrm { c e } } } } { \\mathrm { a r e a } } = 2 \\pi r h + \\pi r ^ { 2 } = \\pi r ( 2 h + r ) .",
          "image_source": "images/page_5_equation_17.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.154,
            0.724,
            0.53,
            0.744
          ],
          "content": "The volume V of the cylinder is"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.287,
            0.749,
            0.708,
            0.768
          ],
          "content": "V = cross-sectional area × height = πr<sup>2</sup>h."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.117,
            0.781,
            0.7,
            0.824
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "14.2.1 Another Way of Understanding Why the Above Formulas Are True"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.115,
            0.835,
            0.745,
            0.912
          ],
          "content": "To understand why the formulas for the surface area (curved part) and the volume of a cylinder hold, it helps to think of the cylinder as a large number of thin circular khakhras stacked on one another."
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.761,
            0.751,
            0.912,
            0.893
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_5_image_22.jpg"
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
